    {
      "type": "calculator",
      "content": "Convert Triple Point Temperature and Pressure",
      "inputs": [
        {
          "id": "temp_celsius",
          "label": "Temperature",
          "type": "number",
          "defaultValue": 0.01,
          "unit": "°C"
        },
        {
          "id": "pressure_kpa",
          "label": "Pressure",
          "type": "number",
          "defaultValue": 0.611657,
          "unit": "kPa"
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "id": "temp_kelvin",
          "label": "Temperature",
          "unit": "K",
          "formula": "inputs.temp_celsius + 273.15",
          "format": "decimal"
        },
        {
          "id": "temp_fahrenheit",
          "label": "Temperature",
          "unit": "°F",
          "formula": "inputs.temp_celsius * 9/5 + 32",
          "format": "decimal"
        },
        {
          "id": "pressure_psi",
          "label": "Pressure",
          "unit": "psi",
          "formula": "inputs.pressure_kpa * 0.145",
          "format": "decimal"
        },
        {
          "id": "pressure_atm",
          "label": "Pressure",
          "unit": "atm",
          "formula": "inputs.pressure_kpa / 101.325",
          "format": "decimal"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "## Additional Information\n\n**Conversion Factor:** 1 kPa (10³ Pa) = 0.145 psi\n\nThe triple point is **not the same** as the [critical point](critical-point-d_997.html). The critical point represents the temperature and pressure above which distinct gas and liquid phases do not exist, whereas the triple point is where all three phases coexist in equilibrium."
    }
  ]
